--- !u!114 &1872530904
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 1872530896}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 502753b90519a664780ac465e908a56f, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  infoPanel: {fileID: 1859622039}
  rockNameText: {fileID: 562214614}
  slot1Text: {fileID: 1673251299}
  slot2Text: {fileID: 1439178974}
  slot3Text: {fileID: 415886847}
  purityText: {fileID: 1581634632}
  rockImage: {fileID: 62265916}
  closeButton: {fileID: 422845550}
  rockInfos:
  - tag: Basalt
    displayName: Basalt
    image: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 0e71b7dfede599941a22a1195ead9a22, type: 3}
    slot1Info: Basalt is the main volcanic rock on Mars. It formed when lava cooled
      and solidified on the surface. On Mars, the crust and many rocks are primarily
      basaltic, just like many lava flows on Earth. This is evidence that Mars had
      ancient volcanic activity, even though today most volcanoes are no longer active.
    slot2Info: 'Basalt underlies more of Earth''s surface than any other rock type.
      Most areas within Earth''s ocean basins are underlain by basalt. Although basalt
      is much less common on continents, lava flows and flood basalts underlie several
      percent of Earth''s land surface. '
    slot3Info: Basalt contains minerals like pyroxene and olivine, which are rich
      in iron and magnesium. These minerals absorb and reflect light differently
      than oxidised red dust, giving basalt a dark gray or blue-gray appearance,
      especially when freshly exposed.
    unlockedLevel: 0
  - tag: CarbonateRock
    displayName: Carbonate Rock
    image: {fileID: 21300000, guid: ea10fce1beb5c5c4cb5ed3d216ca266b, type: 3}
    slot1Info: Carbonate rocks on Mars are made of carbonate minerals that formed
      when rock interacted with water and carbon dioxide. They are usually light-coloured
      and are found in ancient Martian terrain.
    slot2Info: The presence of carbonate rocks suggests Mars once had liquid water
      and a thicker atmosphere. These rocks likely formed in lakes or shallow water
      environments billions of years ago, indicating a warmer and wetter past.
    slot3Info: Carbonate rocks can preserve chemical evidence of past life and help
      stabilise water-friendly conditions. Their discovery on Mars suggests the planet
      may once have been habitable for microbial life.
    unlockedLevel: 0
  - tag: Gypsum
    displayName: Gypsum
    image: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 894722ade7f65b04781ecc131231af60, type: 3}
    slot1Info: "This rock contains gypsum, a soft mineral made of calcium, sulfate,
      and water.\r\n Gypsum forms when salty water evaporates, leaving mineral crystals
      behind.\r\n Finding gypsum here means this rock was shaped by liquid water,
      not just wind or lava.Its presence here shows that Mars once had standing liquid
      water, not just frozen ice."
    slot2Info: "Gypsum dissolves easily in water, so it only forms and survives in
      calm, long-lasting water conditions.\r\n This suggests the area was once part
      of a stable lake or shallow water basin, where water remained for extended
      periods and slowly evaporated.\r\n Such environments allow sediments to settle
      and build up over time."
    slot3Info: "On Earth, gypsum-rich environments often support microbial life and
      preserve chemical traces for billions of years.\r\n These minerals can trap
      and protect biosignatures, making them valuable records of past habitability.\r\n
      If life ever existed on Mars, gypsum-bearing rocks like this are among the
      best candidates for preserving its evidence."
    unlockedLevel: 0
  - tag: Regolith
    displayName: Regolith
    image: {fileID: 21300000, guid: b1bb51278adcca0428d8fcf3e70aa94b, type: 3}
    slot1Info: "Regolith contains large amounts of iron oxide. This resource is what
      gives Mars its signature \u201Creddish\u201D color. "
    slot2Info: It also contains high levels of Perchlorate containing Chlorine, which
      has proven to be toxic to both humans and plants.
    slot3Info: 'Research on Earth has found that a species of plant, Eichhornia crassipes,
      seem to be resistant to the toxins and could be used to remove them in future
      terraforming projects. Regolith is also being researched for In-Situ Resource
      Utilisation(ISRU),which is the practice of using and processing materials found
      on astronomical objects like Mars to replace materials on Earth, to extract
      the oxygen from the abundant oxides. '
    unlockedLevel: 0
  - tag: SmeciteClay
    displayName: Smecite Clay
    image: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 5d860093bf7343d47a60d84cdfc7a5ad, type: 3}
    slot1Info: 'Smectite clay is a green or greenish-grey, fine-grained mineral made
      of layered silicates that forms when rock interacts with water over long periods.
      On Mars, it is linked to ancient wet environments. '
    slot2Info: 'On Mars, smectite clay formed when liquid water slowly altered volcanic
      rock over long periods. This suggests stable, long-lasting water rather than
      brief floods or sudden, extreme events. '
    slot3Info: "Smectite clay forms in mild water conditions and can preserve chemical
      traces over billions of years. This makes it a key material for studying Mars\u2019
      past climate and potential habitability. "
    unlockedLevel: 0
  - tag: Water
    displayName: Water
    image: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 79576f3ff6d470b4385d96273180c8c3, type: 3}
    slot1Info: 'On Mars, water is usually frozen as ice or exists as vapour in the
      air. Any liquid water is extremely salty, unstable, and cannot remain on the
      surface for long. '
    slot2Info: 'Liquid water on Mars exists primarily as briny, salty water that
      flows in seasonal streaks, which is far saltier than the Dead Sea on Earth. '
    slot3Info: "Studying Martian water helps scientists understand Mars\u2019 climate
      history and identify regions that may once have supported life or could support
      future missions. "
    unlockedLevel: 0
  fadeDuration: 0.5
